  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "ES-C55-si-futuro",
      "sequence": 2220,
      "title": "si + futuro",
      "headword": "si + futuro",
      "romanization": "si + futuro",
      "gloss": "if this happens, that will happen — the half of a condition that looks forward",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:9a1e57ae01b7cc04",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say \"if I have time, I speak Spanish.\" (Si tengo tiempo, hablo español.) Two presents."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Now move the second half into the future, where most real conditions actually live."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the present stays put",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "si + present, then future."
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                ""
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 2,
              "utterances": [
                "Si tengo tiempo, hablaré español., If I have time, I will speak Spanish.",
                "Si llueve, comeré en casa., If it rains, I will eat at home."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The result half moves to the future. The si half does not. Spanish will not put a future tense after a si that sets a condition — si tendré tiempo… is simply not said."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "(The other si, the one meaning whether, is a different word doing a different job, and it takes the future quite happily. You will meet it later.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And you already obey that rule in English without having been taught it. Nobody says if it will rain, I won't go. English keeps the present after if too, and neither language explains why."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A condition is not a prediction."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The si half is not claiming the thing will happen — it is setting it aside as a supposition, and a supposition has no tense to be right or wrong about. Only the result half makes a claim about the future, so only the result half gets the future tense."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That is why the rule feels arbitrary and is not: the two halves of the sentence are doing different jobs."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
